+The reusable `workspace/replay_clipped_copy_shortcut.py` confirms this with ten
+protected Racket evaluations and independent Python meanings. Exact programs,
+canonical pairs, native outputs, one-cell difference, source hashes, and runtime
+identity are retained in `checks/306513227c6e/clipping-replay.json`, with wrapper
+receipt `clipping-replay-command.json` and log `clipping-replay.log`.
+
+Ordinary generation only admits in-bounds shifted copies, and `self_check`
+rejects this existing canonical query both for having five rather than exactly
+four motif cells and for its out-of-bounds shifted copy. A bounded Python census
+of six ordinary draws (easy/medium/hard, seeds 0 and 17, sample index 0) observed
+no clipped source and no rival disagreement. These draws were not assembled as
+a lesson; the demonstrated fit and failure use actual canonical demos/query.
+No handcrafted validator case, full battery, or universal sampling claim is used.
+
+There is also a concrete description mismatch: the production summary says
+markers 2 and 3 stay, while the selected authority constructs a blank grid and
+retains only sources 4 and copies 8. Native canonical outputs remove the markers.
+Production, canonical authority, approval, and index were left unchanged. A
+repair would need to teach clipped-copy cases while preserving all original
+source cells, review motif-count support against canonical examples, and correct
+the marker description.
